---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Mission"
  description="Why the New Bill of Rights Foundation exists: the four pillars, the full-spectrum test, and how we work."
>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Mission</h1>

    <h2>Who we are</h2>
    <p>
      The New Bill of Rights Foundation is a nonpartisan organization dedicated
      to passing a new group of amendments for the United States Constitution —
      to secure the future of the American experiment by strengthening four
      things:
    </p>
    <ul class="pillars">
      <li><strong>Freedom</strong> — the individual liberties that define American life</li>
      <li><strong>Democracy</strong> — government that is genuinely accountable to the people</li>
      <li><strong>Rule of Law</strong> — the guarantee that every official, including the president, is bound by the law and the courts</li>
      <li><strong>Good Government</strong> — institutions that are effective, honest, and trustworthy</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Founding principles</h2>

    <h3>Bipartisanship as a fundamental, not a tactic</h3>
    <p>
      Constitutional change in America requires overwhelming consensus —
      two-thirds of Congress and three-fourths of the states. We treat that
      high bar not as an obstacle but as our organizing principle.
    </p>

    <h3>The full-spectrum test</h3>
    <p>
      Our amendments must appeal to the shared values of Americans across the
      entire political spectrum — from MAGA Republicans to socialist Democrats
      and everyone in between. If a proposal cannot win support across that
      full range, it does not belong in our platform. We seek the principles
      that nearly all Americans already agree on, even when their parties do
      not.
    </p>
    <p>
      And we treat the test as a measurement, not a hope. Every proposal is
      judged by its <strong>bipartisan floor</strong> — the level of support in
      the <em>less</em> supportive party — using independent polling, real
      ballot results, and cross-party votes. Proposals that fail the test are
      <a href="/evidence/rejected">set aside publicly, with their evidence</a>,
      and can return only when the evidence changes.
    </p>

    <h3>Only problems that threaten our democracy</h3>
    <p>
      We do not pursue amendments for policy preferences, partisan advantage,
      or symbolic wins. We include only amendments that solve problems that
      genuinely threaten the future of our country. The Constitution is
      amended rarely and for the ages — our proposals must meet that standard.
    </p>

    <h3>Rules that bind everyone</h3>
    <p>
      Every amendment we advance is written behind a veil of ignorance: it
      binds all future presidents, all future majorities, and both parties
      equally, forever. We never cite one side's abuses without the other's,
      and we ask each side to give up a weapon at the same moment the other
      side does.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="veil">
      <p>
        Consensus is possible, not because Americans happen to agree, but
        because no one knows who a fair rule will bind next — and a fair rule
        protects everyone from everyone.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>How we work</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Evidence before advocacy.</strong> We research each focus area
        in depth — the legal landscape, the polling with party breakdowns, the
        state models, and how peer democracies solved the same problem — and we
        publish what fails our bar alongside what passes it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Principles, not machinery.</strong> Constitutional text should
        carry durable principles: rights, limits, and duties that hold for a
        century. Dollar amounts, thresholds, procedures, and agency designs
        belong in legislation — so our drafts state the principle, delegate the
        machinery to Congress, and include express savings clauses protecting
        speech, press, petition, and mercy.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ratify consensus; don't manufacture it.</strong> Amendments
        succeed when they lock in agreement the country has already reached —
        through state constitutions, court decisions, ballot measures, and
        bipartisan votes. Our near-term work of research, polling, and salons
        is how that consensus gets built and proven before the amendment is
        asked to carry it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What success looks like</h2>
    <p>
      A ratified set of amendments — a New Bill of Rights — that strengthens
      the foundations of American freedom, democracy, and self-government for
      generations to come. It will be achieved not by one faction defeating
      another, but by Americans of every political persuasion recognizing their
      shared stake in the country's future. What ratification would change in
      the law already on the books — and what it would leave to Congress and
      the states — is set out in
      <a href="/amendments/after-ratification">After ratification</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="next">
      <a href="/amendments">Read the amendments →</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

